# The other half of the erasure invariant (#86): the Kotlin surface must SURVIVE the erasure, at the head position
# as well as nested. A top-level `T?` param and a top-level `T?` return both become `object`, so a reader has two
# things to recover and needs both channels — and this document is the witness that neither is dropped.
#
#  * [KotlinNullableGeneric] carries the pre-erasure TypeNode, but a reader strips its OUTER nullability (the carrier
#    owns the inner tree, the slot's NRT byte owns the outer `?`), so on its own it restores a NON-null `T`.
#  * the NullableAttribute(2) byte is what puts the `?` back. It cannot come from the ordinary decl-position byte
#    walk, which runs after the erasure and would walk `object` — a non-null default that stamps nothing at all. It
#    is computed from the PRE-erasure type by the recorder.
#
# Drop either and the slot re-imports as a non-null `Any`: a cross-module CONSUMER stops compiling, which no
# runtime-shaped gate can see.
+"name":"DotKt.Runtime.CompilerServices.KotlinNullableGenericAttribute"
+"name":"System.Runtime.CompilerServices.NullableAttribute"},"args":[{"value":2,"type":{"t":"fqn","name":"System.Byte"}}]
# The param and the return are reached by different walks and can regress apart, so both slots are pinned as erased.
+"name":"x","type":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}
